FOMC Preview
2:00am HKT, Thu, Jun 18
This FOMC would be Warsh=92s first meeting as Chair, and will also include =
an updated SEP. A hold at 3.50% =96 3.75% is the consensus base case, leavi=
ng limited event risk around the rate decision itself. The main focus will =
therefore be the tone of the press conference and if Warsh would submit his=
 SEP and dot plot projection.
US Economics: Since Apr FOMC meeting, economic indicators show growing infl=
ation risks, while labor markets have stabilized, current data print is har=
d support hike or cut for this meeting.

  *   Labor: Jobless claims and the JOLTS layoff rate have remained low, wh=
ile hiring activity appears to pick up. NFP headline 3m avg rise to 188k. T=
he unemployment rate has stayed within a narrow range of 4.3-4.4%. Wage gro=
wth has moderated gradually.
  *
Inflation: New sources of price pressure are emerging. The AI Capex investm=
ent has been pushing up prices for consumer electronics and related product=
s. Transportation costs goes higher due to higher fuel costs. Moreover, the=
 spillover effect from energy shocks caused by the conflict in the Middle E=
ast have exerted inflationary pressures on plastic products and other basic=
 materials.
The political pressures eased: President Trump mentioned =93I want Warsh to=
 do whatever he wants=94 in truth social, in a separate interview, Trump do=
 realize that it=92s not reasonable to ease monetary policy in the short te=
rm. https://fortune.com/2026/05/18/trump-interview-economic-strategy-tariff=
s-ai-dealmaking-china-summit/

The committee=92s statement should shift in a slightly hawkish direction, t=
hey may delete =93additional adjustments=94 in =93In considering the extent=
 and timing of additional adjustments to the target range for the federal f=
unds rate, the Committee will carefully assess incoming data, the evolving =
outlook, and the balance of risks.=94 Or at least adjust it to remove the e=
asing bias.
SEP & Dot Plot: I do not expect any near-term change to the format of the S=
EP & Dot plot, as any adjustment to this communication tool would likely re=
quire broader Committee deliberation. Contrary to what some in the market m=
ay expect, my personal view is Warsh would still submit his own projections=
. Although he has previously criticized the Fed=92s use of forward guidance=
, refusing to submit projections at his first meeting could be interpreted =
as a confrontational dissent against his own Committee. That would not be h=
elpful for his longer-term leadership or for managing consensus within the =
FOMC.

  *   Consider the lagged effect from energy shocks caused by Iran war and =
price pressures stemming from the AI Capex investment, I expect the revisio=
n would focus on inflation outlook, while real GDP and UE revise down with =
the negative impact for growth from Iran conflict.

Scenario: How would market react to if Warsh submit his projection?
Scenario
What happens
Market interpretation
Surprise for future rates
Base case: Warsh does not submit SEP/dot; Fed still releases normal SEP/dot=
 from others
Warsh argues: =93I just arrived/I don=92t want to provide forward guidance"
This confirms his anti-forward-guidance stance but does not reveal his rate=
 view. Market focuses instead on median dot, statement language, and press =
conference.
Neutral / slight hawkish via uncertainty
Warsh submits full SEP + dot, and his dot is near the median (no super dovi=
sh points like Miran's)
He participates like a normal Fed Chair. If the median dot shows no 2026 cu=
ts / hold through 2026, his dot is hard to identify.
This is a institutional-continuity signal. It says he is not hiding a very =
dovish Trump aligned dot and is willing to live inside the committee framew=
ork.
Mild hawkish surprise, because submitting at all removes some =93Warsh may =
be secretly dovish=94 optionality.
Warsh submits, and the new distribution suggests his dot is materially belo=
w median (dovish points like Miran's)
A low dot appears, especially if it resembles Miran=92s prior dovish outlie=
r.
This would immediately raise the question: is Warsh genuinely dovish on inf=
lation, or politically aligned with Trump=92s preference for lower rates?
Strong dovish surprise for rate path: front-end rallies, cuts reprice in, U=
SD likely softer; but credibility and inflation-risk premium could complica=
te long-end.
Warsh submits SEP but not dot
He gives GDP / unemployment / inflation projections but refuses to provide =
a policy-rate path.
Warsh participates in macro forecasting but rejects the rate-signaling part=
. However, the dot is what markets care about most, this still looks like a=
n attempt to reduce guidance.
Mild hawkish, because it preserves right-tail hike uncertainty rather than =
giving markets a lower-rate anchor.
Warsh pushes to delay/suspend the entire SEP or dot plot
Not just his own dot: the whole communication tool is delayed, revised, or =
removed.
Biggest communication-regime shock, but eliminating SEP and dot plot would =
require broad consensus, which is unlikely to happen.
Hawkish via term premium, because the Fed removes a rate-path anchor while =
inflation is still above target.

Press Conference: If Warsh were to convey an explicit easing bias, it would=
 immediately raise questions about his credibility as a neutral messenger f=
or the Committee and weaken his authority at the start of his Chairmanship.=
 Instead, he is more likely to play it straight: the Committee has no stron=
g policy bias for now, and policy will remain data dependent as it balances=
 two-sided risks to the employment and inflation mandates.

  *   On inflation, Warsh may try to move the discussion away from a narrow=
 focus on core PCE and highlight alternative measures, such as trimmed-mean=
 PCE. He has historically been sympathetic to trimmed-mean measures, as the=
y are designed to filter out volatile components and provide a smoother rea=
d on underlying inflation.
  *
The latest PCE data showed headline PCE at 3.8% YoY and core PCE at 3.3% Yo=
Y, both the highest since 2023. Core PCE has also risen for three consecuti=
ve months, suggesting that underlying inflation pressure has not yet clearl=
y moderated. By contrast, the Dallas Fed trimmed-mean PCE edged lower to 2.=
35%, moving in the opposite direction from core PCE.
  *
The issue is methodological. Trimmed-mean PCE ranks PCE components by month=
ly price changes, removes the most extreme upside and downside moves, and t=
hen recalculates inflation from the remaining components. This works well w=
hen extreme price moves are mostly noise. But in an environment where the i=
nflation distribution is skewed to the upside (energy, tariffs, insurance, =
medical services or other broad-based price pressures), trimming out the up=
per tail can mechanically understate true inflation pressure.
  *
Therefore, Warsh may find it difficult to persuade the Committee to shift t=
oward trimmed-mean PCE as the main inflation benchmark. For the press confe=
rence, the more credible message would be that the Fed is monitoring a rang=
e of inflation measures, but that the recent rise in core PCE still argues =
against validating near-term easing expectations.
Dissents: There may be dissents: Hawkish Dissent: Logan, Hammack, according=
 to their recent speech and political standing showed below.
Trade Idea: SFRZ6Z7 Flattener


Fed Member Stance: The most hawkish voices, including Logan, Hammack, Schmi=
d, Cook, Waller and Collins, are focused on upside inflation risks, insuffi=
cient policy restrictiveness, and the need to keep further tightening on th=
e table. Williams, Daly, Jefferson, Barkin and Kashkari are closer to neutr=
al, emphasizing patience, data dependence and a wait-and-see approach. Ther=
e are only pockets of dovishness, mainly around Williams=92 relatively beni=
gn inflation view and Bowman, Jefferson, Cook and Goolsbee=92s concerns abo=
ut labor-market downside risks.

Policy Outlook
Date
Speaker
Stance
Key Quotes
3-Jun
Logan
Hawkish
These conditions indicate that monetary policy is not restraining the econo=
my. I am increasingly concerned that higher interest rates could be necessa=
ry later this year to fully restore price stability and appropriately balan=
ce both sides of the Fed=92s dual mandate.
3-Jun
Williams
Neutral
Monetary policy is exactly in the right place. I don=92t see any need to ra=
ise or lower interest rates right now=85 I also don=92t see an obvious dire=
ction where we would go in the future.
2-Jun
Hammack
Hawkish
For today, it=92s reasonable to keep rates steady given the uncertainties a=
round the economic outlook. But if recent trends continue, it may soon be a=
ppropriate to act. Based on the data, I=92m more concerned about the growin=
g risks of persistently elevated inflation than the risks to full employmen=
t and also that monetary policy may not be sufficiently restrictive to brin=
g inflation down to 2%. If we wait for definitive evidence that high inflat=
ion has become embedded in the economy, it may require larger policy adjust=
ments, at greater cost.
29-May
Bowman
Hawkish-leaning
1. As I think about the path forward for monetary policy, I would like to h=
ave more clarity on the economic impacts from the conflict in the Middle Ea=
st and the durability of those effects. In this regard, I appreciated and s=
upported keeping the language from the March post-meeting statement about a=
dditional policy rate adjustments. For now, our moderately restrictive poli=
cy stance is intended to help maintain stable labor market conditions while=
 allowing inflation to resume its downward trend toward 2% once the effects=
 of tariffs and oil prices dissipate.
2. The longer the conflict persists, the more we should consider the effect=
s on inflation in our outlook. In particular, the more persistent higher oi=
l prices are=97or if we start to see broader effects of higher energy price=
s on PCE inflation=97the more likely I will consider shifting my approach t=
o thinking about the balance of risks.
29-May
Daly
Neutral
There=92s no urgency to make any adjustment now... Policy is in a good plac=
e from where we can learn more about the economy.
29-May
Kashkari
Neutral
I think it=92s premature for me to conclude we need to be raising rates rig=
ht away=85 We need to keep watching the data and watching how the conflict =
in the Middle East unfolds before we want to make any adjustment.
29-May
Schmid
Neutral
Once we see how the data come in, then I think we need to have at least the=
 optionality to go up or down relative to the current policy rate.
28-May
Musalem
Hawkish
At the April FOMC, I supported the policy decision but I thought the easing=
 bias was no longer consistent with the outlook and the balance of risks, w=
hich in my view have slightly shifted towards the inflation side.
27-May
Jefferson
Neutral
I believe this policy stance leaves us well positioned to respond to econom=
ic developments based on the incoming data, the evolving outlook, and the b=
alance of risks.
27-May
Cook
Hawkish-leaning
1. I see elevated risks to both sides of our mandate, and from a risk-manag=
ement perspective, I currently believe that the right course of action is t=
o hold rates steady. However, I want to be clear about my risk assessment: =
The risks remain tilted toward higher inflation.
2. I am prepared to raise rates, if the expected disinflation does not appe=
ar in a timely manner. Likewise, I will continue to monitor labor-market de=
velopments, as well, and would be prepared to adjust my policy stance downw=
ard should the labor market deteriorate.
22-May
Waller
Hawkish-leaning
1. So, taking into consideration my evaluation of inflation and the labor m=
arket as well as the risks to the FOMC's price-stability and employment goa=
ls, I do not expect to support a change to the policy rate in the near term=
. The next move, whether it is a hike or cut, will depend on the data. Remo=
ving the language about the extent and timing of additional adjustments wou=
ld make this point clear.
2. I am prepared to be patient in holding policy at its current restrictive=
 setting as we watch how the conflict evolves and what impact there is on i=
nflation and inflation expectations. If I believe inflation expectations st=
art to become unanchored, I would not hesitate to support an increase in th=
e target range for the federal funds rate. But at this point that action is=
 premature. It is time to simply sit and watch how the conflict and the dat=
a evolve.
21-May
Barkin
Neutral
At our last meeting, we held rates steady; with little clarity on the durat=
ion and impact of this latest supply shock, it made sense to give ourselves=
 some time before setting sail. Going forward, I wouldn=92t be surprised if=
 we continue to see rough seas that pressure the employment side of our man=
date, the inflation side of our mandate, or conceivably both. If we do, the=
 Fed is well positioned to respond as appropriate.
19-May
Paulson
Hawkish-leaning
1. I believe monetary policy is in a good place now=85 In my view, monetary=
 policy is mildly restrictive and that restrictiveness is helping to keep t=
he effects of both tariffs and the price increases associated with the conf=
lict in the Middle East in check.
2. Assuming the labor market remains in balance, rate cuts would only becom=
e appropriate once we have seen sustained progress on inflation. However, I=
 think it is healthy that market participants have taken on board scenarios=
 where the funds rate remains unchanged for an extended period, as well as =
scenarios where further tightening becomes necessary.
13-May
Collins
Hawkish-leaning
1. Given this outlook and the balance of risks, I believe it will likely be=
 important to maintain the current slightly restrictive monetary policy sta=
nce for some time. More than five years of above target inflation has reduc=
ed my patience for =93looking through=94 another supply shock.
2. And while it is not in my most likely outlook, I could envision a scenar=
io in which some policy tightening is needed to ensure that inflation retur=
ns durably to 2% in a timely manner. At the moment, though, I see the stanc=
e of monetary policy as well positioned to adjust to the evolving outlook a=
nd balance of risks.

