29-May
Bowman
Dovish
While the labor market appears to have become more stable in recent months,=
 there are still signs of fragility. The unemployment rate remained at 4.3%=
 in April, with stronger payroll employment gains so far this year, but oth=
er indicators continue to show signs of labor market softening.
27-May
Jefferson
Neutral
The U.S. labor market is broadly stable, with both hiring and firing at rel=
atively low levels. I see risks to the labor market as somewhat skewed to t=
he downside.
27-May
Cook
Dovish
In contrast to inflation, the labor market appears to be largely stable=85 =
However, I view the downside risks to the labor market as being elevated.
22-May
Waller
Hawkish
After worrying about a deterioration in the labor market through most of 20=
25, recent data have indicated to me that labor supply and demand have fina=
lly come into a rough balance=85 Despite my lingering concerns going forwar=
d, I don't see the prospect of a weakening labor market as the dominant for=
ce that should be guiding monetary policy in the months ahead.
19-May
Paulson
Hawkish
1. Still, the unemployment rate has been remarkably steady and near what I =
consider to be full employment for many months.
2. My base case is that the labor market will remain stable and that we wil=
l end the year with an unemployment rate in the range that has prevailed ov=
er the past few years.
18-May
Goolsbee
Neutral
The labor market might not be good, but it=92s been stable.





