<p class=3D"MsoNormal" role=3D"presentation" style=3D"text-align: left; tex=
t-indent: 0px; line-height: 18.4px; text-transform: none; margin-top: 0in; =
margin-bottom: 8pt;">
<span style=3D"text-transform: none;">On inflation, Warsh may try to move t=
he discussion away from a narrow focus on core PCE and highlight alternativ=
e measures, such as trimmed-mean PCE. He has historically been sympathetic =
to trimmed-mean measures, as they
 are designed to filter out volatile components and provide a smoother read=
 on underlying inflation.</span></p>
</li><li style=3D"font-family: Aptos, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; color: r=
gb(0, 0, 0); align-self: start; margin-right: 0in; margin-left: 0in;">
<div role=3D"presentation" style=3D"text-align: left; text-indent: 0px; lin=
e-height: 18.4px; margin-top: 0in; margin-bottom: 8pt;">
<span style=3D"text-transform: none;">The latest PCE data showed headline P=
CE at 3.8% YoY and core PCE at 3.3% YoY, both the highest since 2023. Core =
PCE has also risen for three consecutive months, suggesting that underlying=
 inflation pressure has not yet clearly
 moderated. By contrast, the Dallas Fed trimmed-mean PCE edged lower to 2.3=
5%, moving in the opposite direction from core PCE.</span></div>
</li><li style=3D"font-family: Aptos, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; color: r=
gb(0, 0, 0); align-self: start; margin-right: 0in; margin-left: 0in;">
<div role=3D"presentation" style=3D"text-align: left; text-indent: 0px; lin=
e-height: 18.4px; margin-top: 0in; margin-bottom: 8pt;">
<span style=3D"text-transform: none;">The issue is methodological. Trimmed-=
mean PCE ranks PCE components by monthly price changes, removes the most ex=
treme upside and downside moves, and then recalculates inflation from the r=
emaining components. This works well
 when extreme price moves are mostly noise. But in an environment where the=
 inflation distribution is
<b>skewed to the upside (energy, tariffs, insurance, medical services or ot=
her broad-based price pressures)</b>, trimming out the upper tail can mecha=
nically understate true inflation pressure.</span></div>
</li><li style=3D"font-family: Aptos, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt; color: r=
gb(0, 0, 0); align-self: start; margin-right: 0in; margin-left: 0in;">
<div role=3D"presentation" style=3D"text-align: left; text-indent: 0px; lin=
e-height: 18.4px; margin-top: 0in; margin-bottom: 8pt;">
<span style=3D"text-transform: none;">Therefore, </span><span style=3D"back=
ground-color: rgb(255, 255, 0); text-transform: none;"><b>Warsh may find it=
 difficult to persuade the Committee to shift toward trimmed-mean PCE as th=
e main inflation benchmark.</b></span><span style=3D"text-transform: none;"=
>&nbsp;For
 the press conference, the more credible message would be that the Fed is m=
onitoring a range of inflation measures, but that the recent rise in core P=
CE still argues against validating near-term easing expectations.</span></d=
iv>
</li></ul>
